/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  postDict -- the ammonia plant's sizing -> costing -> economics chain.
    1. sizing     -- the two COMPRESSORS (by shaft power), the two HEAT
                     EXCHANGERS (by area), and -- through the `vessel` sizer on
                     an AUTHOR-DECLARED basis -- the CONVERTER (catalyst volume)
                     and the two flash DRUMS (vapour residence).  The
                     equilibrium units emit no volume of their own, which is
                     correct: an equilibrium reactor has no length scale.  The
                     two STORES (DEV.md 4c C92): T101, the syngas surge, as
                     pressure vessels on a pressure swing (`gasStorage`), and
                     T201, the liquid product, as pressurised bullets
                     (`liquefiedGasStorage`); both read their hold time off
                     their flowsheet unit and are costed by their shell steel.
                     What is NOT sized: the mixer and the purge splitter (a
                     tee and a pipe junction -- no vessel, no area, no driver).
    2. costing    -- Guthrie/Turton purchased + bare-module cost, CEPCI-updated
                     to 2026, in EUR.  The syngas compressor is far above the
                     correlation's 3000 kW range -> the pass flags the
                     extrapolation.
    3. economics  -- revenue (NH3), raw-material (syngas makeup), utilities
                     (compressor electricity + cooling water), DCF -> NPV, IRR,
                     payback.  Prices in constant/economics (refuse if absent).

  READ THIS ABOUT THE RETURNS.  The OPEX + revenue side is realistic and
  feedstock-DOMINATED (raw materials the bulk of COM_d -- exactly why ammonia
  margins track the gas price).

  WHAT STAGE OF DESIGN THIS IS, said where a reader audits rather than left to
  be inferred.  The economics block declares `estimateClass 4` -- AACE 18R-97's
  STUDY OR FEASIBILITY class, 1-15 % of complete project definition, method
  "equipment factored or parametric", and the converter is sized by exactly
  that method: a SPACE VELOCITY applied to this run's own throughput,
  DECLARED in grammar the engine parses --

      spaceVelocity 20000;  spaceVelocityBasis normal;  flowKey N_in_mol_s;

  so V = Q_normal(inlet) / SV, with Q_normal the converter INLET gas at
  273.15 K and 101325 Pa (ideal gas), the convention published GHSVs use,
  and the volume moves with the flowsheet instead of standing still while
  it does.  Until 2026-10-05 the volume was a TYPED 80 m3 under a comment
  claiming ~20000 Nm3/(m3 h); measured, 80 m3 at this run's inlet was
  11 360 Nm3/(m3 h), and Vitor ruled that the case declare the rule instead
  (DEV.md C35 item 9).  The 20000 is an ASSUMED feasibility figure, not a
  datum for this catalyst.  It is the first of the three volumes a
  converter gets in a real project -- space velocity at feasibility, an
  integrated rate law with an effectiveness factor at FEED, a licensor's
  guaranteed charge at detailed design -- and it is enough to cost a vessel and
  not enough to build one.  The design sheet says so in its own `basis` field
  ("catalyst V = Q_normal/SV ..."), and this paragraph says why that is the
  RIGHT answer at this class rather than a gap.  Read `ammonia03_quench_converter` for the
  opposite posture: it declares no volume, refuses to cost the converter, and
  says that inventing one converts `unsized` into `falsely sized`.  Both are
  honest; they answer different questions.

  With the sized loop units costed -- and the high-pressure CONVERTER + SEPARATOR
  (thick ASME walls at 220 bar, F_P ~ 36) now DOMINATING the CAPEX -- the
  in-battery-limits FCI is ~55 MEUR (it was ~67 MEUR with the typed 80 m3
  converter); the loop shows NPV ~ +104 MEUR at 10%, IRR ~ 34%, payback
  ~ 3.6 yr (the economics KPIs as of 2026-10-05; the golden is the
  authority, these words are not) -- healthy because the NH3-vs-syngas
  price spread here is a mid-cycle teaching figure and the syngas island's
  capital sits outside the battery limits.  What remains OUTSIDE this
  battery limit is the upstream SYNGAS ISLAND (steam reformer + air separation)
  + the refrigeration package -- a grassroots single-train plant is ~300-500 MEUR
  of total FCI.  So this is a faithful SYNTHESIS-LOOP appraisal, honest that the
  syngas generation is a separate (larger) investment.  Within the loop, every
  unit operation carries its own sizing and cost -- the doctrine Vitor set.

  THE STORES MOVE THAT PICTURE, and by how much is the lesson (DEV.md 4c
  C92; as of 2026-10-18, the golden is the authority).  Priced by their
  shell steel at the declared 8 EUR/kg, the half hour of SYNGAS costs about
  as much as the two days of PRODUCT and more: four 364 m3 vessels with
  200 mm walls (about 14 MEUR) to hold 35 t of gas, against seven 690 m3
  bullets with 43 mm walls (about 10 MEUR) for 2900 t of liquid ammonia.  The
  grassroots factors apply on top, so the FCI rises from ~55 to ~95 MEUR and
  the NPV falls from ~+99 to ~+15 MEUR (IRR ~12 %, discounted payback
  ~12 yr).  A gas is a costly thing to store; that is why the flowsheet's
  T101 comment says real plants take syngas by pipeline, and why deleting
  T101 (and this `gasStorage` entry) is a design decision worth defending.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
sizing
{
    units
    (
        { unitName makeupComp;  type compressor;  material SS316;
          designRules { pressureDesign 250.0; } }
        { unitName recycleComp; type compressor;  material SS316;
          designRules { pressureDesign 205.0; } }
        { unitName FEHE;        type shellTubeHX;  material SS316;
          designRules { U 550.0;  LMTD 150.0;  pressureDesign 220.0; } }
        { unitName waterCooler; type shellTubeHX;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { U 700.0;  LMTD 60.0;   pressureDesign 200.0; } }

        // The CONVERTER: a high-pressure catalyst vessel.  The volume is NOT
        // here; the RULE is (see the header): V = Q_normal(inlet) / SV.
        // Sized as a tall thick-wall vessel at 220 bar.
        { unitName converter;   type vessel;  material SS316;
          designRules { spaceVelocity 20000;        // 1/h, Nm3 gas / (m3 catalyst h) -- ASSUMED
                        spaceVelocityBasis normal;  // the basis the number is quoted on
                        flowKey N_in_mol_s;         // GHSV is on the INLET gas
                        L_over_D 6.0;  pressureDesign 220.0;
                        corrosionAllow 0.004; } }

        // The high-pressure SEPARATOR: a knock-out drum, ~45 s of vapour holdup.
        { unitName separator;   type vessel;  material SS316;
          designRules { residenceTime 45.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;  pressureDesign 220.0; } }

        // The product LET-DOWN drum at 20 bar (smaller, carbon steel).
        { unitName letdown;     type vessel;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { residenceTime 60.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;  pressureDesign 25.0; } }
    );

    //  THE SYNGAS SURGE (DEV.md 4c C92).  T101 holds half an hour of the
    //  25 bar makeup (system/flowsheetDict).  A vessel kept at the line's
    //  own pressure gives back NOTHING without falling below the line, so
    //  the half hour is stored on a PRESSURE SWING: charged up to
    //  maxPressure, drawn down to minPressure.  `unit T101;` reads the hold
    //  time and the stream off the unit (one home, C87); the swing, the
    //  vessels and the price are declared here.  How pointless a store at
    //  the line's pressure would be is the lesson: at 30 bar the same half
    //  hour needs about fifteen times the vessel volume it needs at 100.
    gasStorage
    (
        { unit            T101;
          material        carbonSteel;   // hydrogen service NOT checked (the record carries no limit)
          maxPressure     100 bar;       // absolute: the carbonSteel record's maxP -- AUTHOR-SET
          minPressure     27 bar;        // absolute: the 25 bar makeup line + 2 bar for the let-down valve -- AUTHOR-SET
          temperature     300 K;         // the stream's own: uninsulated vessels -- AUTHOR-SET
          reason          "half an hour of makeup at full rate rides through a trip of the front end long enough to bring the loop down in order; a longer stock of syngas is not stored but supplied by pipeline";
          maxVesselVolume 400;           // m3 per vessel, inside the vessel cost correlation's range
          L_over_D 3;  corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0;   // the drums' constants
          steelPrice      8;             // EUR per kg of SHELL steel, INSTALLED -- AUTHOR-SET
          steelPriceReason "a round placeholder for a fabricated, installed carbon-steel pressure vessel per kg of its shell, heads, nozzles and supports folded in; not a quotation and not taken from any source (the green ammonia PEQ case uses the same figure) -- replace it with a vendor's price";
        }
    );

    //  THE PRODUCT STORE (DEV.md 4c C92).  T201 holds two days of the
    //  let-down drum's liquid (system/flowsheetDict), and that liquid is NOT
    //  pure ammonia: it leaves the drum saturated with the H2, N2 and Ar
    //  dissolved in it at 20 bar, so its BUBBLE pressure at 250 K is the
    //  drum's own 20 bar, where pure ammonia alone would boil at about
    //  1.7 bar.  The run computes both and prints them.  Stored as it
    //  arrives -- 250 K, under pressure -- it needs pressure vessels; an
    //  atmospheric tank would need a second let-down that degasses it (and
    //  refrigeration to keep it at -33 C), which this flowsheet does not
    //  have.  `unit T201;` reads the hold time, the fill fraction and the
    //  stream off the unit; the vessels lie as horizontal bullets on the plot
    //  plan (system/layoutDict).
    liquefiedGasStorage
    (
        { unit            T201;
          material        carbonSteel;   // as the let-down drum at the same 250 K; low-temperature toughness NOT checked
          designPressure  22 bar;        // absolute: 1.1 x the drum's 20 bar, above the liquid's bubble pressure -- AUTHOR-SET
          temperature     250 K;         // the stream's own: insulated vessels -- AUTHOR-SET
          reason          "the liquid is stored as the let-down drum delivers it, at 250 K and above its 20 bar bubble pressure, so nothing flashes in the vessel; insulated bullets keep it there";
          maxVesselVolume 700;           // m3 per bullet -- AUTHOR-SET: a large pressurised bullet; outside the Turton vessel range (0.3-520 m3), which prices only the printed comparison
          L_over_D 6;  corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0;   // a bullet: long and slender -- AUTHOR-SET
          steelPrice      8;             // EUR per kg of SHELL steel, INSTALLED -- AUTHOR-SET
          steelPriceReason "a round placeholder for a fabricated, installed carbon-steel pressure vessel per kg of its shell, heads, nozzles and supports folded in; not a quotation and not taken from any source (the green ammonia PEQ case uses the same figure) -- replace it with a vendor's price";
        }
    );
}
costing
{
    method   Turton;
    year     2026;
    cepci    820;
    cepci2001 397;
    usdToEur 0.92;
}
economics
{
    method              discountedCashFlow;
    projectLife         15;
    constructionPeriod  2;
    //  How the FCI is spent over the two construction years, at the END of
    //  each (DEV.md 4c C63).  AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION, not a datum for this
    //  plant: it is the two-year example distribution CAPCOST 2017 (the
    //  costing program of Turton et al.) ships with.  Until 2026-10-11 the
    //  engine discounted this case over ONE construction year whatever the
    //  line above said; it honours the two years now, so the NPV is the
    //  one this declaration describes.
    capitalSchedule     ( 0.6 0.4 );
    discountRate        0.10;
    taxRate             0.21;
    depreciation        straightLine;
    depreciableLife     9;
    salvageFraction     0.0;
    workingCapital      0.15;
    streamFactor        0.90;
    siteFactor          0.50;
    contingencyFee      1.18;
    estimateClass       4;
    refuseOnMissingPrice 1;

    //  THE LAND (DEV.md 4c C82): the plot area the layout publishes
    //  (system/layoutDict, `plot { margin; }` included) priced as
    //  non-depreciable capital, bought at the start of construction and
    //  recovered at its purchase value at the end of the project.  The price
    //  is a ROUND PLACEHOLDER FOR THE STUDENTS' SITE; NOT A MARKET QUOTATION.
    land
    {
        price   50;     // EUR per m2 of plot
        source  "placeholder for the students' site; not a market quotation";
        reason  "a round number so that the land appears in the capital and the cash flow; replace it with the price of the site the group chooses";
    }
}
